# DURABLE REPRO — A2: a PROJECTION arg passed through a `Callable`-PARAM
# SEGFAULTS. Sibling of A1 (`sound_callable_amp_param_indirect_call.gg`), and a
# DISTINCT costume: A1 is a Callable-typed LOCAL bound to an `&`-param function;
# here the Callable arrives as a PARAMETER and the `&`-arg is a PROJECTION
# (`&c.fd`) rather than a whole local.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, C backend): `gg check` reports "OK: no
# semantic errors", `gg build` succeeds, and the binary exits **139 (SIGSEGV)**.
# Reproduces at base AND under the `&`-of-projection prototype, so the
# projection fix does not close it.
#
# ⚠ `gg run` MASKS this as exit 1 with no output — it does not propagate the
# child's signal status. Reproduce by BUILDING and running the binary directly,
# or the failure looks like an ordinary build error.
#
# ROOT (re-measured 2026-07-27 — two earlier versions of this header named the
# wrong site, and the version after that pinned it by LINE NUMBER, which has
# since rotted; cite the BRANCH, not the line): the SEGV takes the
# Callable-PARAMETER arm in `src/ir/lowering/exprs/calls.rs` — the
# `if local_type_id == UNIT_TYPE` branch that emits `__callable_N` — NOT the
# `GirType::FnPtr` / `__gorget_closure_call_N` arm.
# Verification command:
#     gg build --emit-gir tests/fixtures/security/sound_callable_amp_param_projection.gg
# prints `fn @with_it(unit, *mut Counter)` with `_1: unit ; f`,
# `_3 = field_load _2.*, 0`, `_4 = call @__callable_1(copy _1, copy _3)` — i.e.
# `&c.fd` was lowered as a VALUE copy of the int and handed to a callee that
# expects a pointer.
#
# ⚠ "IT DISCARDS `arg.node.ownership`" UNDERSTATES IT. That arm actively decides
# pointer-vs-value from the ARGUMENT LOCAL's provenance (`is_param_borrow_unique`
# — forward the pointer only if the argument is itself a borrow parameter),
# rather than from the call-site sigil plus the callee's declared function-type
# parameter. The green cells show the axis (all measured at HEAD, all
# `gg check`-clean):
#   `Callable[void(&int)]` PARAM, `cb(a)` where `a` is an `int &` param -> OK
#   `Callable[int(&Counter)]` PARAM, `f(&c)` where `c` is a `Counter &` param -> OK
#     (UNPINNED: callable_ref_param.gg spells it BARE `f(c)`, pinning only that cell)
#     (that second one is the LIVE, GREEN in-tree `tests/fixtures/callable_ref_param.gg`
#      — it must stay green through any fix)
#   `Callable[void(&int)]` LOCAL or PARAM, `cb(&a)` where `a` is a plain local -> SEGV
# So the axis is whether the ARGUMENT's root is already a pointer.
#
# ⚠ A guard wired only at the FnPtr / `__gorget_closure_call_N` arm LEAVES THIS
# CELL LIVE — that was the first (wrong) reading, and acting on it would be a
# Core #4 instance-fix with the actual instance omitted. Re-derive the arg-loop
# census before wiring anything:
#     grep -n 'lower_call_arg\|lower_expr(ctx, builder, &arg.node.value)' \
#       src/ir/lowering/exprs/calls.rs
#
# INTENDED: the indirect call is an ordinary write-through call, so `bumpret`
# increments the caller's `c.fd` and the program prints 11 then 11 and exits 0.
# (If the language instead decides an `&`-param function may not be reified into
# a `Callable` value, the reference-grade answer is a CHECK-TIME rejection —
# never an accepted program that segfaults. Either way today's behaviour is a
# defect; this fixture asserts the accept-and-run reading, matching its A1
# sibling and the `Callable[int(&int)]` annotation the compiler already accepts.)
#
# ORACLE (ggdef): OUT OF SUBSET — ggdef does not reify a named function into a
# `Callable` value ("unresolved local"). Recorded explicitly per Core #9.
#
# The seed is COMPUTED, not a literal, so no const-fold can elide the call.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (CoW / ownership / High): "A PROJECTION arg through a
# `Callable`-PARAM segfaults".

struct Counter:
    int fd

int seed():
    return 10

int bumpret(int &n):
    n = n + 1
    return n

int with_it(Callable[int(&int)] f, Counter &c):
    return f(&c.fd)

void main():
    Counter c = Counter(seed())
    print(with_it(bumpret, &c))
    print(c.fd)
